<Docs slug="compare-a-stack">
  <p>
    A burst gives you ten versions of one moment, and only one of them is
    worth keeping. A <strong>stack</strong> treats those frames as a single
    photo in the grid, so the folder shows each moment once. The photo on top
    is the <strong>representative</strong>; the rest sit behind it, marked by
    offset cards and a count on the thumbnail.
  </p>

  <h2>Making stacks</h2>
  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>By hand.</strong> Select the frames and choose
      <strong>Group into Stack</strong> from the right-click menu.
      <strong>Unstack</strong> splits them apart again and keeps every photo.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>By capture time.</strong> <strong>Auto Stack by Capture
      Time</strong>, in the grid's Actions menu, groups shots taken within a
      second of each other &mdash; which is what a burst is. It runs only
      when you ask.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stacks live with the folder.</strong> Like ratings and edits,
      each stack is a small file in the <code>.lightphotos</code> folder, so
      it travels with the photos.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choosing the best frame</h2>
  <p>
    Open a stacked photo and click <strong>Compare Photos in Stack</strong>
    on the right-hand rail. The representative stays large on the left; every
    frame in the stack appears as a tile beside it.
  </p>
  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Every tile shows the same spot.</strong> Frames in a burst
      differ in the details &mdash; an eye half-closed, focus a few
      centimetres off &mdash; so the tiles all zoom into one shared focus
      area. Click or drag on the large photo, or drag any tile, to move it to
      the part that matters.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Promote the winner.</strong> Hover a tile and click
      <strong>Set as Representative</strong>. That frame becomes the one the
      grid shows, and the one you edit and export.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rate or reject the rest in place.</strong> Each tile has its own
      stars, flags and a trash button, so you can clear out the misses
      without leaving the comparison.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Click the rail icon again, or go back to the grid, to leave the
    comparison. The stack stays as you left it, with the winner on top and
    ready to <a href="/docs/adjust.html">adjust</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
